  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nWarm weather will remain the rule across central Indiana through \nThursday, as upper level ridging remains in place to the south. Our \nnext significant chances for precipitation will come late Thursday \ninto Friday evening, courtesy of a slow-moving frontal system, after \nwhich a substantial change to far more fall-like conditions is \nexpected.\n\nA few patches of fog are possible again this morning, but \nsignificantly increased high clouds over last night suggest far less \ncoverage and little opportunity for dense fog.\n\nWill carry a mention of isolated sprinkles in the north today, as \nechoes associated with midlevel moisture, subtle upper level \ndisturbances, and a weak jet max aloft slide just to our north, \nthough significant dry air below about 8-12kft will render \nmeasurable precipitation exceedingly unlikely - but a few drops may \nsurvive to the ground.\n\nFor the most part, dry and warm conditions will continue through at \nleast early Thursday as a slow moving upper level low just west of \nthe Four Corners region continues to absorb the remnants of \nHurricane Polo and pushes eastward into the central CONUS. The \nfrontal boundary between this system and another large upper low \nwell to our northeast over the eastern Canadian provinces will be \nheld up both by this slow eastward progression and weakening of the \nclosed upper low, and the influence of the upper ridge over the Gulf \nCoast, but will eventually sag into the area Thursday night into \nFriday, which is when rain chances will be highest. Almost without \nexception, dry weather is expected from late Friday night onward, as \nthe blend adapts to recent model runs depicting strong Canadian high \npressure dropping into the CONUS over the weekend into next week, \nbringing much cooler conditions to the area.\n\nA rumble of thunder or two cannot be ruled out at times mainly \nThursday night, though for the most part, just showers will be \nexpected, though locally heavy downpours will be a modest concern \ngiven precipitable water values near climatological maximum thanks \nin part to the presence of tropical moisture from the Polo remnants. \nRecent drier conditions across central Indiana will help to limit \nhydrologic concerns, however.\n\nIn the wake of the front, the aforementioned two areas of strong \nCanadian high pressure will drop into the region, bringing \nsubstantially cooler air and a return to dry conditions for much of \nthe first full week of October, with max temperatures down as much \nas 15-20 degrees and min temperatures down a solid 10-15 degrees, a \nbit below seasonal normals and far more fall-like than conditions \nexperienced through the first third of the Autumn season. The first \nlow temperatures of the season to drop into the 30s will be possible \nearly next week.",
